next week Britain's nurses will become  the latest group of workers to go on  strike fighting for better pay it's a  landmark moment the first time ever that  UK nurses have walked out but they're  not the only group of workers  complaining that their wages are  unsustainably low border force staff  have now announced a day two will strike  later this month joining rail workers  postal staff teachers and University  lecturers ambulance staff and transport  workers in Parliament the opposition  party challenged the Prime Minister this  morning his transport secretary said  that his Flagship legislation on strikes  this is what he said this morning his  transport secretary might want to listen  to this is clearly is clearly not going  to help with the industrial action we're  facing he should stop grandstanding stop  sitting on his hands get round the table  and resolve these issues the government  has been reasonable it's accepted the  recommendations of an independent paper  body giving pay rise sizes in many cases  higher than the private sector but if  the union leaders to continue to be  unreasonable then it is my duty to take  action to protect the lives and  livelihoods of the British public the  lives and livelihoods of the British  public are being hit by rampant  inflation groceries are 14 more  expensive than a year ago headline  inflation is 11 but most people's wages  are not increasing by the same amount if  at all  an increasing inequality between between  the top wages and the lowest wages so  the highest percentiles and the lowest  percentiles but also the median wages so  it has seen actually an impoverishment  of the middle class the Trade union  unite is currently involved in 22  separate pay disputes it refutes the  idea that companies can't afford to  increase salaries unite research has  shown that over the last year the top  companies in the UK they're profit  margins have increased by two-thirds so  what we actually have now is a crisis of  profiteering a recent UK opinion poll  found 60 percent of people are generally  supportive of workers taking industrial  action especially in the current cost of  living crisis but that figure did vary  sector by sector for example 54  supported nurses going on strike while a  figure dropped to 41 in favor of strikes  by rail workers  strike fatigue is a possibility but  there's little sign of it yet Paul  Brennan Al Jazeera London
 

